course: dictionary
lesson: 6222
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one is meant. Read the words around the word. «Dust mites live in your bed and eat flakes of dead skin.» A mite here is a tiny creature, because it lives in a bed and eats skin.
explain: Now read this: «The poor little mite was shivering in the cold.» Here a mite is a small child that you feel sorry for. Nobody means a creature with eight legs in this sentence, so the clues show you the right meaning.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «It is a mite cold» is an informal, old-fashioned way of saying it is a little bit cold. If the sentence still makes sense with your meaning in place of the word, you have probably found the right one.
fact: A mite is a tiny animal with eight legs, and it is related to spiders, not insects.
fact: «Spiders are arachnids, together with scorpions, ticks and mites.» Arachnids have eight legs.
fact: «The varroa mite, about the size of a pinhead, feeds on bees and spreads bee diseases.»
fact: «Dust mites are tiny relatives of spiders, with eight legs, that live in beds and carpets.» Washing bedding hot helps keep them down.
fact: A mite can also be a small child you feel sorry for, a little bit of something, or a very small old coin.
exercise: In «Dust mites live in your bed», what is a mite? | A very small coin | *A tiny animal with eight legs, related to spiders | A small child you feel sorry for | An insect with six legs
exercise: Grandma says, «The poor little mite», looking at a baby. What does mite mean? | A tiny animal with eight legs | *A small child you feel sorry for | A kind of bee | A very small coin
exercise: How many legs does a mite have? | Four | *Eight | Six | Ten
test: Which group of animals is a mite part of? | *Arachnids, the group that includes spiders | Fish | Birds | Insects
test: «It is a mite cold this morning.» What does a mite mean here? | A lot, very | Never | *A little bit | Snowing heavily
exam: Which words in «Dust mites live in beds and carpets and eat flakes of dead skin» tell you a mite is a creature, not a child? | live in beds | dust | *eat | carpets
